<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Swedish Wismar</h1><p class="st-meta">1648 CE – 1803 CE · capital Wismar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Baltic port in Mecklenburg held by Sweden from the Peace of Westphalia until 1803, when it was pawned to Mecklenburg-Schwerin for a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 gave it the town, the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poel">Poel</a> and the district of Neukloster as an imperial fief. Wismar became one of Sweden's footholds on the southern Baltic, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchies-of-bremen-and-verden">Bremen-Verden</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> into a heavily fortified naval base and placed there, from 1653, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar-tribunal">Wismar Tribunal</a>, the supreme court of appeal for all of Sweden's German possessions. The town kept its Hanseatic council and German law, while a Swedish governor commanded the garrison. Its trade suffered from the wars fought over it, and Danish and Prussian forces besieged it in 1675 and 1711-1716.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1803 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> pawned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wismar">Wismar</a> to the Duke of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a> for 1,250,000 riksdaler for 100 years, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-malmo-1803">Treaty of Malmö</a>. The loan was never repaid, and in 1903 Sweden renounced its rights, leaving Wismar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/swedish-wismar.jpg" alt="Swedish Wismar" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: User: David Newton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naval_ensign_of_Sweden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1648 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wismar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Swedish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wismar, Lordship of Wismar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=swedish-wismar" aria-label="Swedish Wismar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4934 328 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Swedish Wismar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5254 584L5381 440M5254 584L5381 440"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0M5381 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5254 584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5254 584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wismar. <a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=swedish-wismar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

